{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-126526" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given that \n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}xy\\,dx+(x+1)\\,dy={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,{\\int }_{0}^{3}\\boxed{}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta ,\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is the circle ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=9$ oriented counterclockwise, what is the missing part of the equation?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061894/state.json" "file_sha256" "deb63e99fcd830a6b6d813531f63eaadda8fb9ab8ae711d0caf2b326eee99ef8"} {"question" "q-126526" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Green's theorem states that \n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nIn our case, the region $D$ and its boundary $C$ satisfy the conditions of Green's theorem:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061894/assets/e69bc348e36b05955d1eaa9eda4516afe137056ead176a46a4fc11bb887ed166.png)\n\nSince $P(x,y)=xy$ and $Q(x,y)=x+1,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=x,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}xy\\,dx+(x+1)\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(1-x)\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the region $D$ can be represented in polar coordinates as\n\n$$\nΔ={(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 3,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi }.\n$$\n\nThe change of variables formula for plane polar coordinates states that \n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write our double integral as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}(1-x)\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}(1-r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,{\\int }_{0}^{3}r-{r}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061894/state.json" "file_sha256" "deb63e99fcd830a6b6d813531f63eaadda8fb9ab8ae711d0caf2b326eee99ef8"} {"question" "q-126526" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061894/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "d428144398c2df4c1fb1f584ee6735a8ea6f59f3f401c5dcb6b2c1cc75db0fbd"} {"question" "q-126526" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061894/state.json" "file_sha256" "deb63e99fcd830a6b6d813531f63eaadda8fb9ab8ae711d0caf2b326eee99ef8"} {"question" "q-126526" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "1-r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta "] ["math" "1-r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta "] ["math" "r-\\frac{{r}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )}{2}"] ["math" "r-{r}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta "] ["math" "r-{r}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta "]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061894/state.json" "file_sha256" "deb63e99fcd830a6b6d813531f63eaadda8fb9ab8ae711d0caf2b326eee99ef8"} {"question" "q-126526" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "r-{r}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta "] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061894/state.json" "file_sha256" "deb63e99fcd830a6b6d813531f63eaadda8fb9ab8ae711d0caf2b326eee99ef8"} {"question" "q-126539" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given that \n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}x{y}^{2}\\,dx+2{x}^{2}y\\,dy={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,{\\int }_{0}^{6}\\boxed{}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta ,\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is the circle ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=36$ oriented counterclockwise, what is the missing part of the equation?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061894/state.json" "file_sha256" "deb63e99fcd830a6b6d813531f63eaadda8fb9ab8ae711d0caf2b326eee99ef8"} {"question" "q-126539" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Green's theorem states that \n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nIn our case, the region $D$ and its boundary $C$ satisfy the conditions of Green's theorem:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061894/assets/c2718471780b5ea4f4b0298624d14a22efb317ef4e8302b9269eeeec9449ca0d.png)\n\nSince $P(x,y)=x{y}^{2}$ and $Q(x,y)=2{x}^{2}y,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=2xy,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=4xy.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}x{y}^{2}\\,dx+2{x}^{2}y\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(4xy-2xy)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}2xy\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the region $D$ can be represented in polar coordinates as\n\n$$\nΔ={(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 6,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi }.\n$$\n\nThe change of variables formula for plane polar coordinates states that \n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write our double integral as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}2xy\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}2\\cdot r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\cdot r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}2{r}^{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,{\\int }_{0}^{6}{r}^{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061894/state.json" "file_sha256" "deb63e99fcd830a6b6d813531f63eaadda8fb9ab8ae711d0caf2b326eee99ef8"} {"question" "q-126539" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061894/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "d428144398c2df4c1fb1f584ee6735a8ea6f59f3f401c5dcb6b2c1cc75db0fbd"} {"question" "q-126539" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061894/state.json" "file_sha256" "deb63e99fcd830a6b6d813531f63eaadda8fb9ab8ae711d0caf2b326eee99ef8"} {"question" "q-126539" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "1-{r}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )"] ["math" "1-{r}^{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )"] ["math" "r-{r}^{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )"] ["math" "{r}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )"] ["math" "{r}^{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061894/state.json" "file_sha256" "deb63e99fcd830a6b6d813531f63eaadda8fb9ab8ae711d0caf2b326eee99ef8"} {"question" "q-126539" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{r}^{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061894/state.json" "file_sha256" "deb63e99fcd830a6b6d813531f63eaadda8fb9ab8ae711d0caf2b326eee99ef8"} {"question" "q-126542" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given that \n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}y\\,dx-{x}^{2}\\,dy={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,{\\int }_{0}^{4}\\boxed{}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta ,\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is the circle ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=16$ oriented counterclockwise, what is the missing part of the equation?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061894/state.json" "file_sha256" "deb63e99fcd830a6b6d813531f63eaadda8fb9ab8ae711d0caf2b326eee99ef8"} {"question" "q-126542" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Green's theorem states that \n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nIn our case, the region $D$ and its boundary $C$ satisfy the conditions of Green's theorem:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061894/assets/5f7d53fb7692663cca39f718491b4dccb6244031e2b7c75f766b7a8340696d64.png)\n\nSince $P(x,y)=y$ and $Q(x,y)=-{x}^{2},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=1,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=-2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}y\\,dx-{x}^{2}\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-2x-1)\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the region $D$ can be represented in polar coordinates as\n\n$$\nΔ={(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 4,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi }.\n$$\n\nThe change of variables formula for plane polar coordinates states that \n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write our double integral as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}(-2x-1)\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}(-2\\cdot r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -1)\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,{\\int }_{0}^{4}-2{r}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061894/state.json" "file_sha256" "deb63e99fcd830a6b6d813531f63eaadda8fb9ab8ae711d0caf2b326eee99ef8"} {"question" "q-126542" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061894/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "d428144398c2df4c1fb1f584ee6735a8ea6f59f3f401c5dcb6b2c1cc75db0fbd"} {"question" "q-126542" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061894/state.json" "file_sha256" "deb63e99fcd830a6b6d813531f63eaadda8fb9ab8ae711d0caf2b326eee99ef8"} {"question" "q-126542" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-2r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -1"] ["math" "-2r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -1"] ["math" "-2{r}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta "] ["math" "-2{r}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -r"] ["math" "-2{r}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -r"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061894/state.json" "file_sha256" "deb63e99fcd830a6b6d813531f63eaadda8fb9ab8ae711d0caf2b326eee99ef8"} {"question" "q-126542" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-2{r}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -r"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061894/state.json" "file_sha256" "deb63e99fcd830a6b6d813531f63eaadda8fb9ab8ae711d0caf2b326eee99ef8"} {"question" "q-126564" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\boxed{}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta ,\n$$\n\nwhere $F(x,y)=⟨xy-\\sqrt[3]{x+5},\\,x+2y⟩$ and $C$ is the circle ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=4$ oriented counterclockwise, what is the missing part of the equation?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061894/state.json" "file_sha256" "deb63e99fcd830a6b6d813531f63eaadda8fb9ab8ae711d0caf2b326eee99ef8"} {"question" "q-126564" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, notice that we can rewrite our integral as \n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr={∮}_{C}(xy-\\sqrt[3]{x+5})\\,dx+(x+2y)\\,dy.\n$$\n\nGreen's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nIn our case, the region $D$ and its boundary $C$ satisfy the conditions of Green's theorem:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061894/assets/798665574b8f0e11d991bb0d934efd01bdeb65840c2e86498be4edbc59578439.png)\n\nSince $P(x,y)=xy-\\sqrt[3]{x+5}$ and $Q(x,y)=x+2y,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=x,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}(xy-\\sqrt[3]{x+5})\\,dx+(x+2y)\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(1-x)\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the region $D$ can be represented in polar coordinates as\n\n$$\nΔ={(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 2,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi }.\n$$\n\nThe change of variables formula for plane polar coordinates states that \n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write our double integral as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}(1-x)\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}(1-r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,{\\int }_{0}^{2}r-{r}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061894/state.json" "file_sha256" "deb63e99fcd830a6b6d813531f63eaadda8fb9ab8ae711d0caf2b326eee99ef8"} {"question" "q-126564" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061894/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "d428144398c2df4c1fb1f584ee6735a8ea6f59f3f401c5dcb6b2c1cc75db0fbd"} {"question" "q-126564" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061894/state.json" "file_sha256" "deb63e99fcd830a6b6d813531f63eaadda8fb9ab8ae711d0caf2b326eee99ef8"} {"question" "q-126564" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "1-r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta "] ["math" "1-r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta "] ["math" "2r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -{r}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta "] ["math" "r-{r}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta "] ["math" "r-{r}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta "]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061894/state.json" "file_sha256" "deb63e99fcd830a6b6d813531f63eaadda8fb9ab8ae711d0caf2b326eee99ef8"} {"question" "q-126564" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "r-{r}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta "] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061894/state.json" "file_sha256" "deb63e99fcd830a6b6d813531f63eaadda8fb9ab8ae711d0caf2b326eee99ef8"} {"question" "q-126567" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,{\\int }_{0}^{5}\\boxed{}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta ,\n$$\n\nwhere $F(x,y)=⟨{y}^{3}+{e}^{x},\\,\\sqrt[3]{{y}^{2}+10}-{x}^{3}⟩$ and $C$ is the circle ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=25$ oriented counterclockwise, what is the missing part of the equation?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061894/state.json" "file_sha256" "deb63e99fcd830a6b6d813531f63eaadda8fb9ab8ae711d0caf2b326eee99ef8"} {"question" "q-126567" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, notice that we can rewrite our integral as \n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr={∮}_{C}({y}^{3}+{e}^{x})\\,dx+(\\sqrt[3]{{y}^{2}+10}-{x}^{3})\\,dy.\n$$\n\nGreen's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nIn our case, the region $D$ and its boundary $C$ satisfy the conditions of Green's theorem:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061894/assets/3e378b8f7731dbdbc44c232e9b8703067f39a5b3f836934afcbacb57d47972b2.png)\n\nSince $P(x,y)={y}^{3}+{e}^{x}$ and $Q(x,y)=\\sqrt[3]{{y}^{2}+10}-{x}^{3},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=3{y}^{2},\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=-3{x}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}({y}^{3}+{e}^{x})\\,dx+(\\sqrt[3]{{y}^{2}+10}-{x}^{3})\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-3{x}^{2}-3{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the region $D$ can be represented in polar coordinates as\n\n$$\nΔ={(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 5,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi }.\n$$\n\nThe change of variables formula for plane polar coordinates states that \n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write our double integral as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}(-3{x}^{2}-3{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}(-3\\cdot (r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}-3\\cdot (r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2})\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}(-3{r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -3{r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}-3{r}^{3}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,{\\int }_{0}^{5}-3{r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061894/state.json" "file_sha256" "deb63e99fcd830a6b6d813531f63eaadda8fb9ab8ae711d0caf2b326eee99ef8"} {"question" "q-126567" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061894/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "d428144398c2df4c1fb1f584ee6735a8ea6f59f3f401c5dcb6b2c1cc75db0fbd"} {"question" "q-126567" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061894/state.json" "file_sha256" "deb63e99fcd830a6b6d813531f63eaadda8fb9ab8ae711d0caf2b326eee99ef8"} {"question" "q-126567" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-3r"] ["math" "-3{r}^{2}"] ["math" "-3{r}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )"] ["math" "-3{r}^{3}"] ["math" "-3{r}^{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061894/state.json" "file_sha256" "deb63e99fcd830a6b6d813531f63eaadda8fb9ab8ae711d0caf2b326eee99ef8"} {"question" "q-126567" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-3{r}^{3}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061894/state.json" "file_sha256" "deb63e99fcd830a6b6d813531f63eaadda8fb9ab8ae711d0caf2b326eee99ef8"} {"question" "q-126710" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061894/assets/831b2131cf134ec21def0014a3121429ded4cd3afd98dfd1660b1e78c1a9ba93.png)\n\nConsider the path $C$ shown above. Given that $F(x,y)=⟨y(x+1),\\,x(y+1)⟩,$ which of the following is equivalent to ${∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr\\,?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061894/state.json" "file_sha256" "deb63e99fcd830a6b6d813531f63eaadda8fb9ab8ae711d0caf2b326eee99ef8"} {"question" "q-126710" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, notice that we can rewrite our integral as \n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr={∮}_{C}y(x+1)\\,dx+x(y+1)\\,dy.\n$$\n\nGreen's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nIn our case, the region $D$ and its boundary $C$ satisfy the conditions of Green's theorem:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061894/assets/d8cd55f88d614bdb49aee6eaa0ff9d1d56f6f597cd892ee199a0130b53954b5b.png)\n\nSince $P(x,y)=y(x+1)$ and $Q(x,y)=x(y+1),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=x+1,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=y+1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}y(x+1)\\,dx+x(y+1)\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(y+1-(x+1))\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(y-x)\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the region $D$ can be represented in polar coordinates as\n\n$$\nΔ={(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 3,\\,\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{3\\pi }{2}}.\n$$\n\nThe change of variables formula for plane polar coordinates states that  \n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write our double integral as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}(y-x)\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{3\\pi /2}\\,{\\int }_{0}^{3}{r}^{2}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )\\,dr\\,d\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061894/state.json" "file_sha256" "deb63e99fcd830a6b6d813531f63eaadda8fb9ab8ae711d0caf2b326eee99ef8"} {"question" "q-126710" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061894/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "d428144398c2df4c1fb1f584ee6735a8ea6f59f3f401c5dcb6b2c1cc75db0fbd"} {"question" "q-126710" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061894/state.json" "file_sha256" "deb63e99fcd830a6b6d813531f63eaadda8fb9ab8ae711d0caf2b326eee99ef8"} {"question" "q-126710" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}\\,{\\int }_{0}^{3}r(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )\\,dr\\,d\\theta "] ["math" "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\,{\\int }_{0}^{3}{r}^{2}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )\\,dr\\,d\\theta "] ["math" "{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{3\\pi /2}\\,{\\int }_{0}^{3}r(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,dr\\,d\\theta "] ["math" "{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{3\\pi /2}\\,{\\int }_{0}^{3}{r}^{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,dr\\,d\\theta "] ["math" "{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{3\\pi /2}\\,{\\int }_{0}^{3}{r}^{2}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )\\,dr\\,d\\theta "]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061894/state.json" "file_sha256" "deb63e99fcd830a6b6d813531f63eaadda8fb9ab8ae711d0caf2b326eee99ef8"} {"question" "q-126710" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{3\\pi /2}\\,{\\int }_{0}^{3}{r}^{2}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )\\,dr\\,d\\theta "] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061894/state.json" "file_sha256" "deb63e99fcd830a6b6d813531f63eaadda8fb9ab8ae711d0caf2b326eee99ef8"} {"question" "q-126709" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061894/assets/008f5dbaeb8c3c26008c49e97ed0490c444b049a991c2198399359a30b806b07.png)\n\nConsider the path $C$ shown above. Given that $F(x,y)=⟨{e}^{x/4}+x{y}^{2},\\,{e}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡y}+2x⟩,$ which of the following is equivalent to ${∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr\\,?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061894/state.json" "file_sha256" "deb63e99fcd830a6b6d813531f63eaadda8fb9ab8ae711d0caf2b326eee99ef8"} {"question" "q-126709" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, notice that we can rewrite our integral as \n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr={∮}_{C}({e}^{x/4}+x{y}^{2})\\,dx+({e}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡y}+2x)\\,dy.\n$$\n\nGreen's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nIn our case, the region $D$ and its boundary $C$ satisfy the conditions of Green's theorem:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061894/assets/c40d9fdfd10b3be4153c9c8ea3a6302af3cf0392533767313c467bdf7dcec1d7.png)\n\nSince $P(x,y)={e}^{x/4}+x{y}^{2}$ and $Q(x,y)={e}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡y}+2x,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=2xy,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}({e}^{x/4}+x{y}^{2})\\,dx+({e}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡y}+2x)\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(2-2xy)\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the region $D$ can be represented in polar coordinates as\n\n$$\nΔ={(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 4,\\,-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le \\theta \\le 0}.\n$$\n\nThe change of variables formula for plane polar coordinates states that \n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write our double integral as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}(2-2xy)\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}(2-2\\cdot r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\cdot r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{0}\\,{\\int }_{0}^{4}(2r-{r}^{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta ))\\,dr\\,d\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061894/state.json" "file_sha256" "deb63e99fcd830a6b6d813531f63eaadda8fb9ab8ae711d0caf2b326eee99ef8"} {"question" "q-126709" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061894/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "d428144398c2df4c1fb1f584ee6735a8ea6f59f3f401c5dcb6b2c1cc75db0fbd"} {"question" "q-126709" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061894/state.json" "file_sha256" "deb63e99fcd830a6b6d813531f63eaadda8fb9ab8ae711d0caf2b326eee99ef8"} {"question" "q-126709" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{0}\\,{\\int }_{0}^{4}(2r-{r}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta ))\\,dr\\,d\\theta "] ["math" "{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{0}\\,{\\int }_{0}^{4}(2r-{r}^{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta ))\\,dr\\,d\\theta "] ["math" "{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{0}\\,{\\int }_{0}^{4}(2r{e}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }-{r}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,dr\\,d\\theta "] ["math" "{\\int }_{0}^{3\\pi /2}\\,{\\int }_{0}^{4}(2r-{r}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,dr\\,d\\theta "] ["math" "{\\int }_{0}^{3\\pi /2}\\,{\\int }_{0}^{4}(r{e}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }-{r}^{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta ))\\,dr\\,d\\theta "]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061894/state.json" "file_sha256" "deb63e99fcd830a6b6d813531f63eaadda8fb9ab8ae711d0caf2b326eee99ef8"} {"question" "q-126709" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{0}\\,{\\int }_{0}^{4}(2r-{r}^{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta ))\\,dr\\,d\\theta "] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061894/state.json" "file_sha256" "deb63e99fcd830a6b6d813531f63eaadda8fb9ab8ae711d0caf2b326eee99ef8"} {"question" "q-126711" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061894/assets/8cb15d895d5baf46d359d811fce99b73238434eb01079d1199cfd99d3f52f222.png)\n\nConsider the path $C$ shown above. Given that $F(x,y)=⟨{y}^{2}+{x}^{2},\\,xy+y{e}^{y}⟩,$ which of the following is equivalent to ${∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr\\,?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061894/state.json" "file_sha256" "deb63e99fcd830a6b6d813531f63eaadda8fb9ab8ae711d0caf2b326eee99ef8"} {"question" "q-126711" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, notice that we can rewrite our integral as \n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr={∮}_{C}({y}^{2}+{x}^{2})\\,dx+(xy+y{e}^{y})\\,dy.\n$$\n\nGreen's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nIn our case, the region $D$ and its boundary $C$ satisfy the conditions of Green's theorem:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061894/assets/232ced663dcac27ce2775af66dd2ef215ed937e0f4eb5e3e6fb008a3d82dc069.png)\n\nSince $P(x,y)={y}^{2}+{x}^{2}$ and $Q(x,y)=xy+y{e}^{y},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=2y,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}({y}^{2}+{x}^{2})\\,dx+(xy+y{e}^{y})\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(y-2y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}-y\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the region $D$ can be represented in polar coordinates as\n\n$$\nΔ={(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 1,\\,-\\pi \\le \\theta \\le 0}.\n$$\n\nThe change of variables formula for plane polar coordinates states that \n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write our double integral as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}-y\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}-r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi }^{0}\\,{\\int }_{0}^{1}-{r}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,dr\\,d\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061894/state.json" "file_sha256" "deb63e99fcd830a6b6d813531f63eaadda8fb9ab8ae711d0caf2b326eee99ef8"} {"question" "q-126711" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061894/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "d428144398c2df4c1fb1f584ee6735a8ea6f59f3f401c5dcb6b2c1cc75db0fbd"} {"question" "q-126711" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061894/state.json" "file_sha256" "deb63e99fcd830a6b6d813531f63eaadda8fb9ab8ae711d0caf2b326eee99ef8"} {"question" "q-126711" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{0}\\,{\\int }_{0}^{1}-{r}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,dr\\,d\\theta "] ["math" "{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{0}\\,{\\int }_{0}^{1}-{r}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,dr\\,d\\theta "] ["math" "{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{0}\\,{\\int }_{0}^{1}r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,dr\\,d\\theta "] ["math" "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\,{\\int }_{0}^{1}-r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,dr\\,d\\theta "] ["math" "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\,{\\int }_{0}^{1}{r}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,dr\\,d\\theta "]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061894/state.json" "file_sha256" "deb63e99fcd830a6b6d813531f63eaadda8fb9ab8ae711d0caf2b326eee99ef8"} {"question" "q-126711" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{0}\\,{\\int }_{0}^{1}-{r}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,dr\\,d\\theta "] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061894/state.json" "file_sha256" "deb63e99fcd830a6b6d813531f63eaadda8fb9ab8ae711d0caf2b326eee99ef8"} {"question" "q-126514" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061894/assets/c1f8bb7ed82d345f8397ea9b01854f05f3f668acdcebf5d75ebbc5cb8de04fa6.png)\n\nConsider the path $C$ shown above. Which of the following is equivalent to the line integral \n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)-{x}^{2}y)\\,dx+(x{y}^{2}-\\frac{1}{y+1})\\,dy\\,?\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061894/state.json" "file_sha256" "deb63e99fcd830a6b6d813531f63eaadda8fb9ab8ae711d0caf2b326eee99ef8"} {"question" "q-126514" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nIn our case, the region $D$ and its boundary $C$ satisfy the conditions of Green's theorem:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061894/assets/9eceb8ab173995a8325fb1a8452baaa7f1071e569391e9a49b69c047d76f3b52.png)\n\nSince $P(x,y)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)-{x}^{2}y$ and $Q(x,y)=x{y}^{2}-\\frac{1}{y+1},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=-{x}^{2},\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}={y}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)-{x}^{2}y)\\,dx+(x{y}^{2}-\\frac{1}{y+1})\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}({y}^{2}-(-{x}^{2}))\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the region $D$ can be represented in polar coordinates as\n\n$$\nΔ={(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 3,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le \\pi }.\n$$\n\nThe change of variables formula for plane polar coordinates states that \n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write our double integral as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}({r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\,{\\int }_{0}^{3}{r}^{3}\\,dr\\,d\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061894/state.json" "file_sha256" "deb63e99fcd830a6b6d813531f63eaadda8fb9ab8ae711d0caf2b326eee99ef8"} {"question" "q-126514" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061894/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "d428144398c2df4c1fb1f584ee6735a8ea6f59f3f401c5dcb6b2c1cc75db0fbd"} {"question" "q-126514" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061894/state.json" "file_sha256" "deb63e99fcd830a6b6d813531f63eaadda8fb9ab8ae711d0caf2b326eee99ef8"} {"question" "q-126514" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}\\,{\\int }_{0}^{3}r\\,dr\\,d\\theta "] ["math" "{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}\\,{\\int }_{0}^{3}{r}^{3}\\,dr\\,d\\theta "] ["math" "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\,{\\int }_{0}^{2}(r+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ))\\,dr\\,d\\theta "] ["math" "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\,{\\int }_{0}^{3}r\\,dr\\,d\\theta "] ["math" "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\,{\\int }_{0}^{3}{r}^{3}\\,dr\\,d\\theta "]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061894/state.json" "file_sha256" "deb63e99fcd830a6b6d813531f63eaadda8fb9ab8ae711d0caf2b326eee99ef8"} {"question" "q-126514" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\,{\\int }_{0}^{3}{r}^{3}\\,dr\\,d\\theta "] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061894/state.json" "file_sha256" "deb63e99fcd830a6b6d813531f63eaadda8fb9ab8ae711d0caf2b326eee99ef8"} {"question" "q-108544" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061894/assets/d0964ff8219d84181163537f5660fe2b504668b1e97e3461cc8cb43d18fc6091.png)\n\nConsider the path $C$ shown above. Which of the following is equivalent to the line integral \n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}(3y-{e}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x})\\,dx+(7x+\\sqrt{y+5})\\,dy\\,?\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061894/state.json" "file_sha256" "deb63e99fcd830a6b6d813531f63eaadda8fb9ab8ae711d0caf2b326eee99ef8"} {"question" "q-108544" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nIn our case, the region $D$ and its boundary $C$ satisfy the conditions of Green's theorem:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061894/assets/deeb5648e328ecc93cf6c1de620e103da0f7677705d1e89e4654deff8d411f66.png)\n\nSince $P(x,y)=3y-{e}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}$ and $Q(x,y)=7x+\\sqrt{y+5},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=3,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=7.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}(3y-{e}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x})\\,dx+(7x+\\sqrt{y+5})\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(7-3)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}4\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the region $D$ can be represented in polar coordinates as\n\n$$\nΔ={(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 2,\\,-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}}.\n$$\n\nThe change of variables formula for plane polar coordinates states that\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write our double integral as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}4\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}4\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}\\,{\\int }_{0}^{2}4r\\,dr\\,d\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061894/state.json" "file_sha256" "deb63e99fcd830a6b6d813531f63eaadda8fb9ab8ae711d0caf2b326eee99ef8"} {"question" "q-108544" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061894/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "d428144398c2df4c1fb1f584ee6735a8ea6f59f3f401c5dcb6b2c1cc75db0fbd"} {"question" "q-108544" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061894/state.json" "file_sha256" "deb63e99fcd830a6b6d813531f63eaadda8fb9ab8ae711d0caf2b326eee99ef8"} {"question" "q-108544" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}\\,{\\int }_{0}^{2}({r}^{3}-{e}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta })\\,dr\\,d\\theta "] ["math" "{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}\\,{\\int }_{0}^{2}4r\\,dr\\,d\\theta "] ["math" "{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta {\\int }_{0}^{2}2r\\,dr\\,d\\theta "] ["math" "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\,{\\int }_{0}^{2}({r}^{3}-{e}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta })r\\,dr\\,d\\theta "] ["math" "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\,{\\int }_{0}^{2}4r\\,dr\\,d\\theta "]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061894/state.json" "file_sha256" "deb63e99fcd830a6b6d813531f63eaadda8fb9ab8ae711d0caf2b326eee99ef8"} {"question" "q-108544" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}\\,{\\int }_{0}^{2}4r\\,dr\\,d\\theta "] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061894/state.json" "file_sha256" "deb63e99fcd830a6b6d813531f63eaadda8fb9ab8ae711d0caf2b326eee99ef8"} {"question" "q-126755" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Use Green's theorem to evaluate ${∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr,$ where $F(x,y)=⟨{4}^{x}-{y}^{3}+2,\\,\\frac{2}{{y}^{2}+1}+{x}^{3}⟩$ and $C$ is the circle ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=2,$ oriented counterclockwise." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061894/state.json" "file_sha256" "deb63e99fcd830a6b6d813531f63eaadda8fb9ab8ae711d0caf2b326eee99ef8"} {"question" "q-126755" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, notice that we can rewrite our integral as \n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr={∮}_{C}({4}^{x}-{y}^{3}+2)\\,dx+(\\frac{2}{{y}^{2}+1}+{x}^{3})\\,dy.\n$$\n\nGreen's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nIn our case, the region $D$ and its boundary $C$ satisfy the conditions of Green's theorem:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061894/assets/c8ebdae5c3fb2c2ca7b318f32a357589d73bbe0d6471877cbf8babd813edd395.png)\n\nNow, since \n\n$$\nP(x,y)={4}^{x}-{y}^{3}+2,\\,Q(x,y)=\\frac{2}{{y}^{2}+1}+{x}^{3},\n$$\n\n we have that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=-3{y}^{2},\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=3{x}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}({4}^{x}-{y}^{3}+2)\\,dx+(\\frac{2}{{y}^{2}+1}+{x}^{3})\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(3{x}^{2}-(-3{y}^{2}))\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}3({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the region $D$ can be represented in polar coordinates as\n\n$$\nΔ={(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le \\sqrt{2},\\,0\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi }.\n$$\n\nThe change of variables formula for plane polar coordinates states that\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nFinally, we can calculate our double integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}3({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}3({r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =3{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}}{r}^{3}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =3{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}}{r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =3{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }[\\frac{{r}^{4}}{4}]_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =3{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =3[\\theta ]_{0}^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =3(2\\pi -0) \\\\  & =6\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061894/state.json" "file_sha256" "deb63e99fcd830a6b6d813531f63eaadda8fb9ab8ae711d0caf2b326eee99ef8"} {"question" "q-126755" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061894/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "d428144398c2df4c1fb1f584ee6735a8ea6f59f3f401c5dcb6b2c1cc75db0fbd"} {"question" "q-126755" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061894/state.json" "file_sha256" "deb63e99fcd830a6b6d813531f63eaadda8fb9ab8ae711d0caf2b326eee99ef8"} {"question" "q-126755" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "3\\pi "] ["math" "6\\pi "] ["math" "\\frac{3\\pi }{2}"] ["math" "\\frac{\\pi }{3}"] ["math" "\\frac{\\pi }{6}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061894/state.json" "file_sha256" "deb63e99fcd830a6b6d813531f63eaadda8fb9ab8ae711d0caf2b326eee99ef8"} {"question" "q-126755" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "6\\pi "] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061894/state.json" "file_sha256" "deb63e99fcd830a6b6d813531f63eaadda8fb9ab8ae711d0caf2b326eee99ef8"} {"question" "q-126759" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Use Green's theorem to evaluate ${∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr,$ where $F(x,y)=⟨\\frac{\\sqrt{x+5}-{y}^{3}}{3},\\frac{\\sqrt{y+5}+{x}^{3}}{3}⟩$ and $C$ is the circle ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=4,$ oriented counterclockwise." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061894/state.json" "file_sha256" "deb63e99fcd830a6b6d813531f63eaadda8fb9ab8ae711d0caf2b326eee99ef8"} {"question" "q-126759" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, notice that we can rewrite our integral as \n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr={∮}_{C}\\frac{\\sqrt{x+5}-{y}^{3}}{3}\\,dx+\\frac{\\sqrt{y+5}+{x}^{3}}{3}\\,dy.\n$$\n\nGreen's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nIn our case, the region $D$ and its boundary $C$ satisfy the conditions of Green's theorem:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061894/assets/09a69771617d73af6e4a1a42720223951e2e6edeb71a8df4d12aeb1bc22031aa.png)\n\nNow, since \n\n$$\nP(x,y)=\\frac{\\sqrt{x+5}-{y}^{3}}{3},\\,Q(x,y)=\\frac{\\sqrt{y+5}+{x}^{3}}{3},\n$$\n\n we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=-{y}^{2},\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}={x}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}\\frac{\\sqrt{x+5}-{y}^{3}}{3}\\,dx+\\frac{\\sqrt{y+5}+{x}^{3}}{3}\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}({x}^{2}-(-{y}^{2}))\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the region $D$ can be represented in polar coordinates as\n\n$$\nΔ={(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 2,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi }.\n$$\n\nThe change of variables formula for plane polar coordinates states that\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nFinally, we can calculate our double integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}({r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{2}{r}^{3}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{2}{r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }[\\frac{{r}^{4}}{4}]_{0}^{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }4\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =4[\\theta ]_{0}^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =4(2\\pi -0) \\\\  & =8\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061894/state.json" "file_sha256" "deb63e99fcd830a6b6d813531f63eaadda8fb9ab8ae711d0caf2b326eee99ef8"} {"question" "q-126759" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061894/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "d428144398c2df4c1fb1f584ee6735a8ea6f59f3f401c5dcb6b2c1cc75db0fbd"} {"question" "q-126759" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061894/state.json" "file_sha256" "deb63e99fcd830a6b6d813531f63eaadda8fb9ab8ae711d0caf2b326eee99ef8"} {"question" "q-126759" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "0"] ["math" "16\\pi "] ["math" "2\\pi "] ["math" "4\\pi "] ["math" "8\\pi "]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061894/state.json" "file_sha256" "deb63e99fcd830a6b6d813531f63eaadda8fb9ab8ae711d0caf2b326eee99ef8"} {"question" "q-126759" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "8\\pi "] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061894/state.json" "file_sha256" "deb63e99fcd830a6b6d813531f63eaadda8fb9ab8ae711d0caf2b326eee99ef8"} {"question" "q-126733" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Use Green's theorem to evaluate ${∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr,$ where $F(x,y)=⟨2-{x}^{2}y,1+x{y}^{2}⟩$ and $C$ is the circle ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=25,$ oriented counterclockwise." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061894/state.json" "file_sha256" "deb63e99fcd830a6b6d813531f63eaadda8fb9ab8ae711d0caf2b326eee99ef8"} {"question" "q-126733" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, notice that we can rewrite our integral as \n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr={∮}_{C}(2-{x}^{2}y)\\,dx+(1+x{y}^{2})\\,dy.\n$$\n\nGreen's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nIn our case, the region $D$ and its boundary $C$ satisfy the conditions of Green's theorem:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061894/assets/d081f4dc19484f0c483c5518295c7c3b7195bb096404fb7ff6e27d8bda1cccc1.png)\n\nNow, since \n\n$$\nP(x,y)=2-{x}^{2}y,\\,Q(x,y)=1+x{y}^{2},\n$$\n\n we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=-{x}^{2},\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}={y}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}(2-{x}^{2}y)\\,dx+(1+x{y}^{2})\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}({y}^{2}-(-{x}^{2}))\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}({y}^{2}+{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the region $D$ can be represented in polar coordinates as\n\n$$\nΔ={(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 5,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi }.\n$$\n\nThe change of variables formula for plane polar coordinates states that\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nFinally, we can calculate our double integral, as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}({r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{5}{r}^{3}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{5}{r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }[\\frac{{r}^{4}}{4}]_{0}^{5}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\frac{625}{4}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{625}{4}[\\theta ]_{0}^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{625}{4}(2\\pi -0) \\\\  & =\\frac{625\\pi }{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061894/state.json" "file_sha256" "deb63e99fcd830a6b6d813531f63eaadda8fb9ab8ae711d0caf2b326eee99ef8"} {"question" "q-126733" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061894/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "d428144398c2df4c1fb1f584ee6735a8ea6f59f3f401c5dcb6b2c1cc75db0fbd"} {"question" "q-126733" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061894/state.json" "file_sha256" "deb63e99fcd830a6b6d813531f63eaadda8fb9ab8ae711d0caf2b326eee99ef8"} {"question" "q-126733" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "625\\pi "] ["math" "\\frac{125\\pi }{2}"] ["math" "\\frac{25\\pi }{4}"] ["math" "\\frac{5\\pi }{4}"] ["math" "\\frac{625\\pi }{2}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061894/state.json" "file_sha256" "deb63e99fcd830a6b6d813531f63eaadda8fb9ab8ae711d0caf2b326eee99ef8"} {"question" "q-126733" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{625\\pi }{2}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061894/state.json" "file_sha256" "deb63e99fcd830a6b6d813531f63eaadda8fb9ab8ae711d0caf2b326eee99ef8"} {"question" "q-126747" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061894/assets/21e8aa9acdca87acf153b2e7ad3f5e51d5708f53b81562e682e16098721d752a.png)\n\nEvaluate ${∮}_{C}({3}^{x}+{x}^{2}y)dx+({2}^{y}+x{y}^{2})\\,dy,$ where the path $C$ is shown above." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061894/state.json" "file_sha256" "deb63e99fcd830a6b6d813531f63eaadda8fb9ab8ae711d0caf2b326eee99ef8"} {"question" "q-126747" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nIn our case, the region $D$ and its boundary $C$ satisfy the conditions of Green's theorem:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061894/assets/922ae907661e21ea5510dc0d02f151afc4f4423ad11a572ede6849c623397baa.png)\n\nNow, since \n\n$$\nP(x,y)={3}^{x}+{x}^{2}y,\\,Q(x,y)={2}^{y}+x{y}^{2},\n$$\n\n we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}={x}^{2},\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}={y}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}({3}^{x}+{x}^{2}y)dx+({2}^{y}+x{y}^{2})\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}({y}^{2}-{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the region $D$ can be represented in polar coordinates as\n\n$$\nΔ={(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 4,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{3}}.\n$$\n\nThe change of variables formula for plane polar coordinates states that\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nFinally, we can calculate our double integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}({y}^{2}-{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}({r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta -{r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}{\\int }_{0}^{4}-{r}^{3}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta ){\\int }_{0}^{4}{r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta ){[\\frac{{r}^{4}}{4}]}_{0}^{4}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =-64{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =-64{[\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )}{2}]}_{0}^{\\pi /3} \\\\  & =-32[\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi }{3})-\\operatorname{sin}⁡0] \\\\  & =-16\\sqrt{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061894/state.json" "file_sha256" "deb63e99fcd830a6b6d813531f63eaadda8fb9ab8ae711d0caf2b326eee99ef8"} {"question" "q-126747" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061894/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "d428144398c2df4c1fb1f584ee6735a8ea6f59f3f401c5dcb6b2c1cc75db0fbd"} {"question" "q-126747" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061894/state.json" "file_sha256" "deb63e99fcd830a6b6d813531f63eaadda8fb9ab8ae711d0caf2b326eee99ef8"} {"question" "q-126747" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-16\\sqrt{3}"] ["math" "-\\frac{8\\pi }{3}"] ["math" "8\\pi "] ["math" "8\\sqrt{3}"] ["math" "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061894/state.json" "file_sha256" "deb63e99fcd830a6b6d813531f63eaadda8fb9ab8ae711d0caf2b326eee99ef8"} {"question" "q-126747" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-16\\sqrt{3}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061894/state.json" "file_sha256" "deb63e99fcd830a6b6d813531f63eaadda8fb9ab8ae711d0caf2b326eee99ef8"} {"question" "q-126736" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Use Green's theorem to evaluate ${∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr,$ where $F(x,y)=⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-2{x}^{2}y,\\text{ }\\,\\sqrt{y+3}+2x{y}^{2}⟩$ and $C$ is the circle ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=1,$ oriented counterclockwise." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061894/state.json" "file_sha256" "deb63e99fcd830a6b6d813531f63eaadda8fb9ab8ae711d0caf2b326eee99ef8"} {"question" "q-126736" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, notice that we can rewrite our integral as \n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr={∮}_{C}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-2{x}^{2}y)\\,dx+(\\sqrt{y+3}-2x{y}^{2})\\,dy.\n$$\n\nGreen's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nIn our case, the region $D$ and its boundary $C$ satisfy the conditions of Green's theorem:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061894/assets/902790a00a049bbfc759403125ad5f19a8c2528a46b59c721ac43fb137cd5d8e.png)\n\nNow, since \n\n$$\nP(x,y)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-2{x}^{2}y,\\,Q(x,y)=\\sqrt{y+3}+2x{y}^{2},\n$$\n\n we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=-2{x}^{2},\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=2{y}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-2{x}^{2}y)\\,dx+(\\sqrt{y+3}-2x{y}^{2})\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(2{y}^{2}-(-2{x}^{2}))\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}2({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the region $D$ can be represented in polar coordinates as\n\n$$\nΔ={(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 1,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi }.\n$$\n\nThe change of variables formula for plane polar coordinates states that\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nFinally, we can calculate our double integral, as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}2({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}2({r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{1}{r}^{3}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{1}{r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }[\\frac{{r}^{4}}{4}]_{0}^{1}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\frac{1}{4}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[\\theta ]_{0}^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061894/state.json" "file_sha256" "deb63e99fcd830a6b6d813531f63eaadda8fb9ab8ae711d0caf2b326eee99ef8"} {"question" "q-126736" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061894/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "d428144398c2df4c1fb1f584ee6735a8ea6f59f3f401c5dcb6b2c1cc75db0fbd"} {"question" "q-126736" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061894/state.json" "file_sha256" "deb63e99fcd830a6b6d813531f63eaadda8fb9ab8ae711d0caf2b326eee99ef8"} {"question" "q-126736" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "2\\pi "] ["math" "\\frac{3\\pi }{2}"] ["math" "\\frac{\\pi }{2}"] ["math" "\\frac{\\pi }{4}"] ["math" "\\pi "]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061894/state.json" "file_sha256" "deb63e99fcd830a6b6d813531f63eaadda8fb9ab8ae711d0caf2b326eee99ef8"} {"question" "q-126736" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\pi "] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061894/state.json" "file_sha256" "deb63e99fcd830a6b6d813531f63eaadda8fb9ab8ae711d0caf2b326eee99ef8"} {"question" "e-9551" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given that\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061894/assets/5bf7df5fffeecf9bdaa888ebe196a194a6b6e24aa69c01ea74ee2aac1a3f4774.png)\nwhere $\\mathbf{F}(x,y) = \\left\\langle y^2+\\sin x, \\: 3xy-\\cos y  \\right\\rangle$ and $C$ is the circle $x^2+y^2=4$ oriented counterclockwise, what is the missing part of the equation?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061894/example-9551.json" "file_sha256" "d96e79f37000410b9770fbbe4a1e8c2d0fda78a4be906de462ab800a166f4319"} {"question" "e-9551" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, notice that we can rewrite our integral as \n\n$$\n\\displaystyle{\\oint\\limits_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot\\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{r}}\n=\n\\oint\\limits_{C} (y^2+\\sin x) \\: \\mathrm{d}x + (3xy-\\cos y) \\: \\mathrm{d}y.\n$$\n\nGreen's theorem states that\n\n$$\n\\oint\\limits_{C} P\\,\\mathrm{d}x + Q\\,\\mathrm{d}y \n= \\iint\\limits_D \\left(\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x} - \\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}\\right)\\,\\textrm d A.\n$$\n\nIn our case, the region $D$ and its boundary $C$ satisfy the conditions of Green's theorem:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061894/assets/798665574b8f0e11d991bb0d934efd01bdeb65840c2e86498be4edbc59578439.png)\n\nSince $P(x,y)= y^2+\\sin x$ and $Q(x,y)= 3xy-\\cos y,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=2y,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=3y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}({y}^{2}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)\\,dx+(xy-\\operatorname{cos}⁡y)\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(3y-2y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}y\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the region $D$ can be represented in polar coordinates as\n\n$$\n\\Delta = \\big\\{ (r,\\theta) \\: : \\: 0\\leq r \\leq 2,\\: 0 \\leq \\theta \\leq 2\\pi  \\big\\}.\n$$\n\nThe change of variables formula for plane polar coordinates states that\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_{D} f(x,y) \\ \\mathrm d A = \\iint\\limits_{\\Delta} \\ f(r \\cos\\theta, r\\sin\\theta) \\:  r \\ \\mathrm d r \\mathrm d \\theta.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write our double integral as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}y\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,{\\int }_{0}^{2}{r}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the missing expression is $r^2\\sin\\theta.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061894/example-9551.json" "file_sha256" "d96e79f37000410b9770fbbe4a1e8c2d0fda78a4be906de462ab800a166f4319"} {"question" "e-9076" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061894/assets/ffaf242238a8d32ce0f134cff6e0cf4256b2888b0a71f65e3c5ec470cbce284a.png)\n\nConsider the path $C$ shown above and the vector field $\\mathbf{F}(x,y) = \\left\\langle 2y + \\ln{(\\sqrt{x+5})}, \\: e^{y/3} + 3x \\right\\rangle.$ \nFind a repeated integral in plane polar coordinates that's equivalent to $\\displaystyle \\oint\\limits_{C} \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{r} \\, .$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061894/example-9076.json" "file_sha256" "c0a21edcd31048219c2671163dd0f3ee862e939d083558ad142e6fac3cbc1ba5"} {"question" "e-9076" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, notice that we can rewrite our integral as \n\n$$\n\\displaystyle{\\oint\\limits_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot\\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{r}}\n=\n\\oint\\limits_{C} (2y + \\ln{(\\sqrt{x+5})}) \\: \\mathrm{d}x + \\left( e^{y/3} + 3x \\right) \\: \\mathrm{d}y.\n$$\n\nGreen's theorem states that\n\n$$\n\\oint\\limits_{C} P\\,\\mathrm{d}x + Q\\,\\mathrm{d}y \n= \\iint\\limits_D \\left(\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x} - \\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}\\right)\\,\\textrm d A.\n$$\n\nIn our case, the region $D$ and its boundary $C$ satisfy the conditions of Green's theorem:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061894/assets/e4c7913793a4b08916a832e575c5b2c167d5dfeec244eea23857731df1e514e7.png)\n\nSince $P(x,y)= 2y + \\ln{(\\sqrt{x+5})}$ and $Q(x,y)=e^{y/3} + 3x,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=2,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}(2y+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\sqrt{x+5}))\\,dx+({e}^{y/3}+3x)\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(3-2)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the region $D$ can be represented in polar coordinates as\n\n$$\n\\Delta = \\left\\{ (r,\\theta) \\: : \\: 0 \\leq r \\leq 3, \\: \\dfrac{\\pi}{2} \\leq \\theta \\leq 2\\pi \\right\\}.\n$$\n\nThe change of variables formula for plane polar coordinates states that\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_{D} f(x,y) \\ \\mathrm d A = \\iint\\limits_{\\Delta} \\ f(r \\cos\\theta, r\\sin\\theta) \\:  r \\ \\mathrm d r \\mathrm d \\theta.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write our double integral as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{2\\pi }\\,{\\int }_{0}^{3}r\\,dr\\,d\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061894/example-9076.json" "file_sha256" "c0a21edcd31048219c2671163dd0f3ee862e939d083558ad142e6fac3cbc1ba5"} {"question" "e-9550" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061894/assets/e12a47b603d2c4193453d0f4daf18f54e52ed973498f520feef32cfafbe28a9f.png)\n\n  \n\nEvaluate ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/140