n-capture-workers/multivariable/14061894/assets/cce6516260d79ee10d0343f341da2b8246ca12c5e9a8ee119d8a25a29476a281.png)\n\nNow, notice that the region ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061894/assets/05ff793b0aee1f05a79216f9e7f119afa544749c81077937f8f06a353f6ef4eb.png) can be represented in polar coordinates as\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061894/assets/5b07149309edcf9c83af0282b7b8e34942ed3e6879d61f549317357d05748a45.png)\n\nThe change of variables formula for plane polar coordinates states that\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061894/assets/530d94860e23561ee4f296c5007cbe30a1d22028deb60fc11cb87cf5d3640497.png)\n\nFinally, we can calculate our double integral, as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061894/assets/c8094d11eab9d06ae1997a2168ec694066f59ea16aa1ec0c4788978012e62022.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061894/example-9550.json" "file_sha256" "25e479c3afcbbc8b139d21555f22111a694966731f93451069b010094b3707ff"}] "usage" {"e-9076" [] "e-9551" [] "e-9550" [] "q-108544" []} "basis" 1985 "retractions" [[17592186176145 :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3627/Source/Images/q-108544.png)\n\nConsider the path $C$ shown above. Which of the following is equivalent to the line integral\n$∮_(C)(3y - e^{\\cos x})dx + (7x + \\sqrt{y + 5})dy$?"] [17592186175802 :question/problem "Given that\n$AAAAAAAA$\nwhere $\\mathbf{F}(x,y) = \\left\\langle y^2+\\sin x, \\: 3xy-\\cos y \\right\\rangle$ and $C$ is the circle $x^2+y^2=4$ oriented counterclockwise, what is the missing part of the equation?"] [17592186175802 :question/worked-solution "First, notice that we can rewrite our integral as\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle{\\oint\\limits_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot\\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{r}} = \\oint\\limits_{C} (y^2+\\sin x) \\: \\mathrm{d}x + (3xy-\\cos y) \\: \\mathrm{d}y\n$$\n\nGreen's theorem states that\n\n$$\n\\oint\\limits_{C} P\\,\\mathrm{d}x + Q\\,\\mathrm{d}y = \\iint\\limits_D \\left(\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x} - \\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}\\right)\\,\\textrm d A\n$$\n\nIn our case, the region $D$ and its boundary $C$ satisfy the conditions of Green's theorem:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3627/Source/Images/629b2ed342ba6afd917f54d19b0f1b7a.png)\n\nSince\n\n$$\nP(x,y)= y^2+\\sin x\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nQ(x,y)= 3xy-\\cos y\n$$\n\nwe obtain\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y} = 2y, \\frac{∂Q}{∂x} = 3y\n$$\n\nApplying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∮_(C)(y^{2} + \\sin x)dx + (xy - \\cos y)dy &= ∬_(D)(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x} - \\frac{∂P}{∂y})dA \\\\\n&= ∬_(D)(3y - 2y)dA \\\\\n&= ∬_(D)ydA\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the region $D$ can be represented in polar coordinates as\n\n$$\n\\Delta = \\big\\{(r,\\theta) \\:: \\: 0\\leq r \\leq 2,\\: 0 \\leq \\theta \\leq 2\\pi \\big\\}\n$$\n\nThe change of variables formula for plane polar coordinates states that\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_{D} f(x,y) \\ \\mathrm d A = \\iint\\limits_{\\Delta} \\ f(r \\cos\\theta, r\\sin\\theta) \\: r \\ \\mathrm d r \\mathrm d \\theta\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write our double integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∬_(D)ydA &= ∬_(Δ)r\\sin θrdrdθ \\\\\n&= ∫_{0}^{2π}∫_{0}^{2}r 2 sin ⁡ θdrdθ\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the missing expression is $r^2\\sin\\theta$."] [17592186175760 :question/problem "Consider the path $C$ shown above and the vector field $\\mathbf{F}(x,y) = \\left\\langle 2y + \\ln{(\\sqrt{x+5})}, \\: e^{y/3} + 3x \\right\\rangle$.\nFind a repeated integral in plane polar coordinates that's equivalent to $\\displaystyle \\oint\\limits_{C} \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{r} \\,$.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3627/Source/Images/f328d63e617baaed38300a4f68a3df63.png)"] [17592186175760 :question/worked-solution "First, notice that we can rewrite our integral as\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle{\\oint\\limits_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot\\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{r}} = \\oint\\limits_{C} (2y + \\ln{(\\sqrt{x+5})}) \\: \\mathrm{d}x + \\left(e^{y/3} + 3x \\right) \\: \\mathrm{d}y\n$$\n\nGreen's theorem states that\n\n$$\n\\oint\\limits_{C} P\\,\\mathrm{d}x + Q\\,\\mathrm{d}y = \\iint\\limits_D \\left(\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x} - \\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}\\right)\\,\\textrm d A\n$$\n\nIn our case, the region $D$ and its boundary $C$ satisfy the conditions of Green's theorem:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3627/Source/Images/e8dfd43252a2b65794b5a03ca7b3995d.png)\n\nSince\n\n$$\nP(x,y)= 2y + \\ln{(\\sqrt{x+5})}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nQ(x,y)=e^{y/3} + 3x\n$$\n\nwe obtain\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y} = 2, \\frac{∂Q}{∂x} = 3\n$$\n\nApplying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∮_(C)(2y + \\ln (\\sqrt{x + 5}))dx + (e^{y/3} + 3x)dy &= ∬_(D)(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x} - \\frac{∂P}{∂y})dA \\\\\n&= ∬_(D)(3 - 2)dA \\\\\n&= ∬_(D)dA\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the region $D$ can be represented in polar coordinates as\n\n$$\n\\Delta = \\left\\{(r,\\theta) \\:: \\: 0 \\leq r \\leq 3, \\: \\dfrac{\\pi}{2} \\leq \\theta \\leq 2\\pi \\right\\}\n$$\n\nThe change of variables formula for plane polar coordinates states that\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_{D} f(x,y) \\ \\mathrm d A = \\iint\\limits_{\\Delta} \\ f(r \\cos\\theta, r\\sin\\theta) \\: r \\ \\mathrm d r \\mathrm d \\theta\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write our double integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∬_(D)dA &= ∬_(Δ)rdrdθ \\\\\n&= ∫_{π/2}^{2π}∫_{0}^{3}rdrdθ\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186175801 :question/problem "Evaluate $\\displaystyle\\oint\\limits_{C} \\left(e^{-x^2}-y^3\\right) \\mathrm{d}x + (e^{-y^2}+x^3) \\: \\mathrm{d}y$, where the path $C$ is shown above.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3627/Source/Images/2e2cf25681d84163eb1887aebdea07b0.png)"] [17592186175801 :question/worked-solution "Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n\\oint\\limits_{C} P\\,\\mathrm{d}x + Q\\,\\mathrm{d}y = \\iint\\limits_D \\left(\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x} - \\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}\\right)\\,\\textrm d A\n$$\n\nIn our case, the region $D$ and its boundary $C$ satisfy the conditions of Green's theorem.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3627/Source/Images/80b659f9413d74a0171fcf45372ad34d.png)\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\nP(x,y) = e^{-x^2}-y^3, \\qquad Q(x,y) = e^{-y^2}+x^3\n$$\n\nwe obtain\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y} =-3y^{2}, \\frac{∂Q}{∂x} = 3x^{2}\n$$\n\nApplying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∮_(C)(e^{-x^{2}} - y^{3})dx + (e^{-y^{2}} + x^{3})dy &= ∬_(D)(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x} - \\frac{∂P}{∂y})dA \\\\\n&= ∬_(D)(3x^{2} - (-3y^{2}))dA \\\\\n&= ∬_(D)3(x^{2} + y^{2})dA\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the region $D$ can be represented in polar coordinates as\n\n$$\n\\Delta = \\Big\\{(r,\\theta) \\:: \\: 0 \\leq r \\leq 2, \\: 0 \\leq \\theta \\leq \\pi \\Big\\}\n$$\n\nThe change of variables formula for plane polar coordinates states that\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_{D} f(x,y) \\ \\mathrm d A = \\iint\\limits_{\\Delta} \\ f(r \\cos\\theta, r\\sin\\theta) \\: r \\ \\mathrm d r \\mathrm d \\theta\n$$\n\nFinally, we can calculate our double integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∬_(D)(3x^{2} + y^{2})dA &= ∬_(Δ)3(r^{2}\\cos^{2} θ + r^{2}\\sin^{2} θ)rdrdθ \\\\\n&= ∫_{0}^{π}∫_{0}^{2}3r^{3}(\\cos^{2} θ + \\sin^{2} θ)drdθ \\\\\n&= ∫_{0}^{π}∫_{0}^{2}3r^{3}drdθ \\\\\n&= ∫_{0}^{π}[\\frac{3r^{4}}{4}]_{0}^{2}dθ \\\\\n&= ∫_{0}^{π}12dθ \\\\\n&= 12[θ]_{0}^{π} \\\\\n&= 12π\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"]] "immutable_answers" [17592186173533 17592186173534 17592186173535 17592186173536 17592186173537] "immutable_fields" [[17592186173532 :answer-field/correct] [17592186173532 :answer-field/id] [17592186173532 :answer-field/key] [17592186173532 :answer-field/type]] "no_history" []}
